Publication‑Ready Abstract — Astrometric Simulation Engine (ASE)

Author: C. Matthew BlackwellSystem: Astrometric Simulation Engine — Proton‑Laser Interaction & Cosmic Data Integration Framework

Abstract

The Astrometric Simulation Engine (ASE) introduces a unified computational architecture that integrates astrophysical environmental datasets with high‑energy proton–laser interaction physics inside a scalable, HPC‑accelerated, zero‑trust‑secured framework. ASE combines heterogeneous cosmic inputs—including HDF5 astrophysics archives, FITS telescope imagery, GEANT4 particle‑interaction datasets, cosmic‑ray spectra, star‑formation models, and interstellar medium (ISM) density maps—into a standardized preprocessing pipeline that constructs physically consistent boundary conditions for microphysical plasma simulations.

At its core, ASE employs a hybrid solver architecture consisting of a Particle‑in‑Cell (PIC) engine, Vlasov–Maxwell field evolution, charge‑conserving Esirkepov/Yee interpolation, relativistic Boris/Yaw particle pushing, and collision operators for particle–field and particle–particle interactions. These solvers are coupled with a magnetohydrodynamic (MHD) module, enabling multi‑scale simulations that capture both local plasma kinetics and large‑scale astrophysical magnetic structures. Proton acceleration mechanisms—including Target Normal Sheath Acceleration (TNSA) and Radiation Pressure Acceleration (RPA)—are modeled within laser–plasma interaction zones whose parameters (pulse duration, intensity, wavelength, focal geometry) are defined through the ingestion layer.

ASE is optimized for distributed high‑performance computing environments using MPI/OpenMP parallelization, CUDA/HIP GPU acceleration, InfiniBand interconnects, and Lustre/GPUDirect storage, supporting ensemble simulations, parameter sweeps, and real‑time cluster orchestration. A zero‑trust security subsystem provides AES‑256 data‑at‑rest encryption, TLS 1.3 data‑in‑transit protection, Merkle‑tree integrity verification, hardware security module (HSM) integration, and role‑based access control (RBAC), ensuring confidentiality and reproducibility in sensitive research workflows.

A Qt/Python‑based Simulation Designer GUI enables interactive configuration, 3D visualization, data slicing, and comparative analysis across cosmic and plasma domains. Together, these components establish ASE as a holistic, multi‑scale simulation platform capable of bridging astrophysical environmental conditions with high‑energy proton–laser physics. The system provides a novel computational foundation for research in cosmic‑ray acceleration, plasma astrophysics, laser‑driven proton dynamics, and high‑energy particle transport across interstellar structures.

